WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.820
Ian: In Marvel's latest
movie, Brie Larson steps into

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.460
the role of Carol Danvers
in "Captain Marvel."

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.140
Like many Marvel
Cinematic Universe movies,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.006
"Captain Marvel" gave us not one

00:00:14.006 --> 00:00:16.864
but two post-credit scenes.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.750
Let's take a look at what we saw.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.143
Oh, and obviously, spoilers ahead.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.130
The first post-credits scene takes place

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:30.720
at some point after the events
of "Avengers: Infinity War."

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.050
After Thanos completes
his Infinity Gauntlet

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.260
and eliminates half of
all life in the universe

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.300
with a snap of his fingers,
the remaining Avengers

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.550
are left to pick up the pieces.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.950
Here, we see Captain
America, Bruce Banner,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.350
Black Widow, and War Machine

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.100
all out of costume in a room together.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.570
They look at a small beeper

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.890
that flashes Captain Marvel's logo.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.850
If you'll recall from
the post-credits scene

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.750
at the end of "Infinity
War," Nick Fury sends out

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.030
a distress call to Captain Marvel.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.163
It rings as he turns to dust.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.030
What other heroes could the Avengers

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.690
be trying to call for help?

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.450
We know that Ant-Man somehow
escapes the Quantum Realm

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.140
and ends up in "Endgame"

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.280
as he's present in the
movie's first teaser trailer.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.010
Ant-Man: Ant-Man? Ant-Man,
I know you know that.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.362
Ian: During this post-credits
scene, the signals go

00:01:19.362 --> 00:01:23.490
blank, then suddenly
Captain Marvel pops up.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.270
"Where's Fury?" she asks

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.960
before the scene cuts to black,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.200
seemingly unaware of what Thanos has done.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.670
This post-credits scene is a
great teaser for "Endgame,"

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:34.840
but it also leaves us with

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.320
a lot more questions than answers.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.890
"Captain Marvel" takes place in the 1990s.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.470
When and where was Captain Marvel

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.860
when she got Fury's distress signal?

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.610
Was she leaping through time and space

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.283
when the Thanos snap happened?

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.120
Now, it's no secret that Captain Marvel

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.020
will meet up with the surviving
Avengers in "Endgame."

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.730
And no, we don't get to meet

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.580
any new characters in this scene.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.990
But getting to see Carol Danvers

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.760
in the same room with them is a huge deal

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.400
and a long time coming.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.900
Nick Fury: Oh, oh, you wanna get personal.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.287
Ian: Following in the
footsteps of a few other Marvel

00:02:09.287 --> 00:02:12.010
movies, the second post-credits
scene in "Captain Marvel"

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.580
is more of a gag, well, a literal gag.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.700
In the first Avengers, the
second post-credits scene

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.290
features the gang eating shawarma

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:22.770
to reward themselves for saving the world.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.770
Iron Man: You ever tried shawarma?

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.177
There's a shawarma joint
about two blocks from here.

00:02:27.177 --> 00:02:28.630
I don't know what it
is, but I wanna try it.

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:29.551
Ian: The second end-credit scene

00:02:29.551 --> 00:02:31.470
of "Guardians of the Galaxy"

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.070
featured a cameo from Howard the Duck.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.230
The second post-credits
scene in "Captain Marvel"

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.030
gives a platform to Goose,
the movie's scene-stealing

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.064
feline who we also find
out is an alien lifeform.

00:02:43.064 --> 00:02:45.380
Fury: Aren't you the cutest little thing?

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.528
Ian: Goose's most dangerous
weapon is a pair of

00:02:47.528 --> 00:02:49.750
tentacles that pop out of his mouth,

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.290
capable of swallowing humans whole.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.070
At one point, he also
swallows the Tesseract,

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.760
which has been an object of
intrigue for many characters

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.270
throughout the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.520
During "Captain Marvel," Nick Fury

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.810
takes quite a liking to Goose

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.080
even though Goose took Fury's eye out,

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.780
which is a drastic change from how

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.220
Fury lost his eye in the comics.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.700
In the comics, he lost his eye

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.730
from a grenade in World War II.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.500
In this scene, Goose jumps
up on Nick Fury's empty desk.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.650
At first, it feels like we're about to see

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.290
a post-Thanos world,
similar to what we saw

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.790
during the end credits of
"Ant-Man and the Wasp."

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.980
This scene shows Goose slowly but surely

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.680
coughing up the Tesseract.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.840
So clearly, this is set not long

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.154
after the events of "Captain Marvel."

00:03:34.154 --> 00:03:37.600
The Tesseract serves an
incredibly important purpose.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.520
It holds the Space Stone.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.500
During its history, it has been possessed

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.650
by a wide array of characters.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.674
In "Captain America: The First Avenger,"

00:03:45.674 --> 00:03:48.100
Red Skull briefly holds it.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.600
Howard Stark then locates
it deep in the ocean

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.550
at the end of that same movie,

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.763
in the wreckage of
Captain America's plane.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.520
In "Thor," it's in SHIELD's possession.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:01.600
Loki briefly gets it in
the first "Avengers."

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.210
Most recently, it was
in Thanos' possession

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.743
along with the rest of
the Infinity Stones.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.560
Between the time Stark has it

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.420
to the time Nick Fury has it,

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.040
it somehow ends up on Mar-Vell's ship.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:20.890
A lot of people thought that Jude Law

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:22.260
would be playing Mar-Vell,

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.180
a Kree spy sent to Earth,
not Annette Bening.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.010
How did she get the Tesseract?

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:31.390
Is it possible that she worked
with Stark on a project,

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:33.230
maybe involving light speed,

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.730
and she was entrusted with the Tesseract?

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.750
But again, we're left with
more questions than answers.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.970
Fury: That's a yes or no question.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:41.803
Carol Danvers: Yes.

00:04:41.803 --> 00:04:43.178
Ian: Overall, the second scene is really

00:04:43.178 --> 00:04:44.353
just there for fun.

00:04:44.652 --> 00:04:46.600
It's a reward for those who keep watching

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.410
after the final credits.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.000
So there you have it.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.410
That's what happened during the two

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:53.483
end-credits scenes in "Captain Marvel."

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.250
Was there anything we missed?

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.803
Let us know in the comments.

